WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.504
Two tasks write a loss over

2
00:00:02.586 --> 00:00:04.300
many samples at once.

3
00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:06.616
The Fréchet distance compares two

4
00:00:06.676 --> 00:00:09.118
sets of images by the mean and covariance

5
00:00:09.178 --> 00:00:10.250
of their features.

6
00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:13.062
The Representation Fréchet Loss makes it

7
00:00:13.123 --> 00:00:15.400
a training loss for image generators.

8
00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:18.114
The statistics must cover many images

9
00:00:18.114 --> 00:00:20.790
but only the current batch has a gradient.

10
00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:22.919
So a feature queue keeps past

11
00:00:22.981 --> 00:00:24.637
features without gradients

12
00:00:24.699 --> 00:00:26.294
and mixes in the new batch

13
00:00:26.356 --> 00:00:27.460
with its gradient.

14
00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:30.409
Our toy generator has one feature

15
00:00:30.409 --> 00:00:32.360
with a target mean of zero.

16
00:00:32.710 --> 00:00:34.899
With a queue of sixteen features

17
00:00:34.966 --> 00:00:36.757
its mean dips only to minus

18
00:00:36.823 --> 00:00:38.150
zero point one nine.

19
00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:40.147
With five hundred twelve

20
00:00:40.147 --> 00:00:42.980
it overshoots to minus one point six four.

21
00:00:43.330 --> 00:00:44.735
A long window lags

22
00:00:44.735 --> 00:00:46.805
it still sees old features

23
00:00:46.805 --> 00:00:48.210
and keeps pushing.

24
00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:50.305
Claude Opus 5.5 found the

25
00:00:50.375 --> 00:00:51.980
same lesson on its own

26
00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:53.935
it replaced the long window

27
00:00:54.005 --> 00:00:55.680
with an exponential one.

28
00:00:56.030 --> 00:00:57.442
It had the best score

29
00:00:57.442 --> 00:01:00.202
seven point two against three point eight

30
00:01:00.202 --> 00:01:01.550
but no model passed.

31
00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:04.756
LeJEPA trains an encoder without labels

32
00:01:04.822 --> 00:01:07.120
two noisy views of one hidden state

33
00:01:07.186 --> 00:01:09.090
should give matching outputs.

34
00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:11.760
Alone, that is easy to cheat

35
00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:14.080
map everything to one point.

36
00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:15.183
In our toy

37
00:01:15.251 --> 00:01:17.783
an encoder shrunk a hundred times has

38
00:01:17.852 --> 00:01:20.110
an alignment loss of almost zero.

39
00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:22.404
So a regularizer, SIGReg

40
00:01:22.482 --> 00:01:24.427
pushes the outputs toward

41
00:01:24.504 --> 00:01:26.060
a standard Gaussian.

42
00:01:26.410 --> 00:01:27.143
In our toy

43
00:01:27.143 --> 00:01:29.410
a whitening loss plays that role

44
00:01:29.410 --> 00:01:32.010
and only the rotation is good on both.

45
00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:34.840
Three of sixteen models passed.

46
00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:37.732
A world model is a learned simulator

47
00:01:37.732 --> 00:01:39.731
given a frame and some actions

48
00:01:39.731 --> 00:01:41.980
it predicts the frames that follow.

49
00:01:42.330 --> 00:01:44.436
Nano World Model uses diffusion

50
00:01:44.502 --> 00:01:46.148
and each future frame has

51
00:01:46.214 --> 00:01:47.530
its own noise level.

52
00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:50.681
One rule covers the common schedules

53
00:01:50.757 --> 00:01:52.725
frame number f starts late

54
00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:54.920
by f times the lag, in rows.

55
00:01:55.270 --> 00:01:57.321
With a lag of the whole schedule

56
00:01:57.321 --> 00:02:00.055
each frame finishes before the next starts

57
00:02:00.055 --> 00:02:02.230
the sequential mode the task uses.

58
00:02:02.810 --> 00:02:05.130
A diffusion sample is random.

59
00:02:05.480 --> 00:02:08.712
Part of its error is shared by every sample

60
00:02:08.712 --> 00:02:09.960
and part is not.

61
00:02:10.310 --> 00:02:12.630
Averaging cancels the second part.

62
00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:14.155
In our toy frame

63
00:02:14.224 --> 00:02:16.642
one sample has a PSNR of twenty-one

64
00:02:16.711 --> 00:02:18.162
point seven decibels

65
00:02:18.231 --> 00:02:20.511
and an average of sixteen reaches

66
00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:22.100
thirty-one point five.

67
00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:25.026
Claude Opus 5.5 did exactly this

68
00:02:25.026 --> 00:02:28.383
sixteen samplers of fifty steps, averaged

69
00:02:28.461 --> 00:02:30.569
then re-noised slightly and

70
00:02:30.647 --> 00:02:32.130
denoised once more.

71
00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:34.314
It was the only model to pass

72
00:02:34.375 --> 00:02:36.453
with eighty-one point four against

73
00:02:36.514 --> 00:02:37.920
seventy-nine point one.

74
00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:41.222
So: in a population loss only the new

75
00:02:41.295 --> 00:02:43.135
batch carries a gradient

76
00:02:43.135 --> 00:02:44.900
and a long window lags.

77
00:02:45.250 --> 00:02:47.810
Matching views needs a regularizer.

78
00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:51.016
And sixteen short samplers, averaged

79
00:02:51.016 --> 00:02:52.560
made the only pass.

80
00:02:52.910 --> 00:02:54.900
Next: two short training runs

81
00:02:54.966 --> 00:02:56.756
and the second easiest task

82
00:02:56.823 --> 00:02:57.950
in the benchmark.
